<article class=3D"single">
  <h2><a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/">My review of the=
 C standard library in practice</a></h2>
  <time datetime=3D"2023-02-11">
    February 11, 2023
  </time>
  <div class=3D"print-only url">
    nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/
  </div>

  <p><em>This article was discussed <a href=3D"https://news.ycombinator.com=
/item?id=3D34752400">on Hacker News</a> and critiqued <a href=3D"https://ut=
cc.utoronto.ca/~cks/space/blog/programming/CaseForAtomicTypes">on
Wandering Thoughts</a>.</em></p>

<p>In general, when working in C I avoid the standard library, libc, as muc=
h
as possible. If possible I won=E2=80=99t even link it. For people not used =
to
working and thinking this way, the typical response is confusion. Isn=E2=80=
=99t
that like re-inventing the wheel? For me, <em>libc is a wheel barely worth
using</em> =E2=80=94 too many deficiencies in both interface and implementa=
tion.
Fortunately, it=E2=80=99s easy to build a better, simpler wheel when you kn=
ow the
terrain ahead of time. In this article I=E2=80=99ll review the functions an=
d
function-like macros of the C standard library and discuss practical
issues I=E2=80=99ve faced with them.</p>

<!--more-->

<p>Fortunately the flexibility of C-in-practice makes up for the standard
library. I already have all the tools at hand to do what I need =E2=80=94 n=
ot
beholden to any runtime.</p>

<p>How does one write portable software while relying little on libc?
Implement the bulk of the program as platform-agnostic, libc-free code
then write platform-specific code per target =E2=80=94 a platform layer =E2=
=80=94 each in
its own source file. The platform code is small in comparison: mostly
unportable code, perhaps <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2015/05/15=
/">raw system calls</a>, graphics functions, or
even assembly. It=E2=80=99s where you get access to all the coolest toys. O=
n some
platforms it will still link libc anyway because it=E2=80=99s got useful
platform-specific features, or because it=E2=80=99s mandatory.</p>

<p>The discussion below is specifically about standard C. Some platforms
provide special workarounds for their standard function shortcomings, but
that=E2=80=99s irrelevant. If I need to use a non-standard function then I=
=E2=80=99m
already writing platform-specific code and I might as well take full
advantage of that fact, bypassing the original issue entirely by calling
directly into the platform.</p>

<p>The rest of this article goes through the standard library listing in <a=
 href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181230041359if_/http://www.open-std.=
org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/abq/c17_updated_proposed_fdis.pdf">the
C18 draft</a> mostly in order.</p>

<h3 id=3D"assert-and-abort">assert and abort</h3>

<p>I <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022/06/26/">wrote about the <=
code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">assert</code> macro las=
t year</a>. While C assertions
are better than the same in any other language I know =E2=80=94 a trap <em>=
without
first unwinding the stack</em> =E2=80=94 the typical implementation doesn=
=E2=80=99t have the
courtesy to trap in the macro itself, creating friction. Or worse, it
doesn=E2=80=99t trap at all and instead exits the process normally with a n=
on-zero
status. It=E2=80=99s not optimized for debuggers.</p>

<p>My non-trivial programs quickly pick up this definition instead, adjuste=
d
later as needed:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#define ASSERT(c) if (!(c)) __b=
uiltin_trap()
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>There=E2=80=99s no diagnostic, but I usually don=E2=80=99t want that any=
way. The vast
majority of the time these are caught in a debugger, and I don=E2=80=99t ne=
ed or
want a diagnostic.</p>

<p>I have no objections to <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">static_assert</code>, but it=E2=80=99s also not part of the
runtime.</p>

<h3 id=3D"math-functions">Math functions</h3>

<p>By this I mean all the stuff in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highli=
ghter-rouge">math.h</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">complex.h</code>, etc. It=E2=80=99s good that
these are, in practice, pseudo-intrinsics. They=E2=80=99re also one of the =
more
challenging parts of libc to replace. It prioritizes precision more than I
usually need, but that=E2=80=99s a reasonable default.</p>

<h3 id=3D"character-classification-and-mapping">Character classification an=
d mapping</h3>

<p>Includes <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isalnum</c=
ode>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isalpha</code>, =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isascii</code>, <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isblank</code>, <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">iscntrl</code>, <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isdigit</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isgraph</code>, <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">islower</code>, <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isprint</code>, <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ispunct</code>, <code class=3D"language=
-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isspace</code>, <code class=3D"language-plain=
text highlighter-rouge">isupper</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isxdigit</code>, <code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tolower</code>, and <code c=
lass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">toupper</code>. The interface=
 is misleading, almost
maliciously so, and these functions are misused in every case I=E2=80=99ve =
seen in
the wild. If you see <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#=
include &lt;ctype.h&gt;</code> in a source file then it=E2=80=99s
probably defective. I=E2=80=99ve been guilty of it myself. When it=E2=80=99=
s up to me,
these functions are banned without exception.</p>

<p>Their prototypes are all shaped like so:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">i=
sXXXXX</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">int</span><span c=
lass=3D"p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>However, the domain of the input is <code class=3D"language-plaintext hi=
ghlighter-rouge">unsigned char</code> plus <code class=3D"language-plaintex=
t highlighter-rouge">EOF</code>. Negative
arguments, aside from <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
EOF</code>, are <em>undefined behavior</em>, despite the obvious
use case being strings. So this is incorrect:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"o">*=
</span><span class=3D"n">s</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"p">...;</span>
<span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">isd=
igit</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">s</span><span class=
=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">]))</span> <spa=
n class=3D"p">{</span>   <span class=3D"c1">// WRONG!</span>
    <span class=3D"p">...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">char</code> is s=
igned, as it is on x86, then it=E2=80=99s undefined for arbitrary
strings, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">s</code>. Som=
e implementations even crash on such inputs.</p>

<p>If the argument was <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>unsigned char</code>, then it would at least truncate into
range, <em>usually</em> leading to the desired result. (Though not so if <a=
 href=3D"https://drewdevault.com/2020/09/25/A-story-of-two-libcs.html">pass=
ing
Unicode code points</a>, which is an odd mistake to make.) Except that
it has to accommodate <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
EOF</code>. Why that? <strong>These functions are defined for
use with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fgetc</code>,=
 not strings!</strong></p>

<p>You could patch over it with truncation by masking:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"p">(</s=
pan><span class=3D"n">isdigit</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">s</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"mi">0</span><span c=
lass=3D"p">]</span> <span class=3D"o">&amp;</span> <span class=3D"mi">255</=
span><span class=3D"p">))</span> <span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"p">...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>However, you=E2=80=99re still left with <em>locales</em>. This is a bit =
of <em>global state</em>
that changes <a href=3D"https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/commit/1e70e82baa=
9193f6f027338b0fab0f5078971fbe">how a number of libc functions behave</a>, =
including
character classification. While locales have some niche uses, most of the
time <a href=3D"https://utcc.utoronto.ca/~cks/space/blog/linux/BashLocaleSc=
riptDestruction?showcomments">the behavior is surprising and undesirable</a=
>. It=E2=80=99s also bad for
performance. I=E2=80=99ve developed a habit of using <code class=3D"languag=
e-plaintext highlighter-rouge">LC_ALL=3DC</code> before some GNU
programs so that they behave themselves. If you=E2=80=99re parsing a fixed =
format
that doesn=E2=80=99t adapt to locale =E2=80=94 virtually everything =E2=80=
=94 you definitely do
not want locale-based character classification of input.</p>

<p>Since the interface and behavior both unsuited for most uses, you=E2=80=
=99re
better off making your own range checks or lookup tables for your use
case. When you name it, probably avoid starting the function with <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">is</code>
since <a href=3D"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20230109-00/?p=
=3D107685">it=E2=80=99s reserved</a>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">_Bool</span> <span class=3D"nf"=
>xisdigit</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <s=
pan class=3D"n">c</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">c</span><span class=
=3D"o">&gt;=3D</span><span class=3D"sc">'0'</span> <span class=3D"o">&amp;&=
amp;</span> <span class=3D"n">c</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span><span=
 class=3D"sc">'9'</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>I used <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">char</code>,=
 but this still works fine for naive UTF-8 parsing.</p>

<h3 id=3D"errno">errno</h3>

<p>Without libc you don=E2=80=99t have to use this global, hopefully thread=
-local,
pseudo-variable. Good riddance. Return your errors, and use a struct if
necessary.</p>

<h3 id=3D"locales">locales</h3>

<p>As discussed, locales have some niche uses =E2=80=94 formatting dates co=
mes to
mind =E2=80=94 but what little use they have is trapped behind global state=
 set by
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">setlocale</code>, maki=
ng it sometimes impossible to use correctly.</p>

<p>On Windows I=E2=80=99ve instead used <a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.=
com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winnls/nf-winnls-getlocaleinfow">GetLocaleInfoW=
</a> to get information like,
=E2=80=9CWhat is the local name of the <a href=3D"https://github.com/skeeto=
/scratch/blob/master/windows/cal.c">current month</a>?=E2=80=9D</p>

<h3 id=3D"setjmp-and-longjmp">setjmp and longjmp</h3>

<p>Sometimes tricky to use correctly, particularly with regard to qualifyin=
g
local variables as <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">vol=
atile</code>. It can compose with region-based allocation
to <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/01/18/#implementation-highl=
ights">automatically and instantly free</a> all objects created between set
and jump. These macros are fine, but don=E2=80=99t overdo it.</p>

<h3 id=3D"variable-arguments">variable arguments</h3>

<p>Variadic functions are occasionally useful, and the <code class=3D"langu=
age-plaintext highlighter-rouge">va_start</code>/<code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">va_end</code>
macros make them possible. These are, unfortunately, notoriously complex
because calling conventions do not go out of their way to make them any
simpler. They <a href=3D"https://blog.nelhage.com/2010/10/amd64-and-va_arg/=
">require compiler assistance</a>, and in practice they=E2=80=99re
implemented as part of the compiler rather than libc. They=E2=80=99re okay,=
 but I
can live without it.</p>

<h3 id=3D"signals">signals</h3>

<p>While important on unix-like systems, signals as defined in the C standa=
rd
library are essentially useless. If you=E2=80=99re dealing with signals, or=
 even
something <em>like</em> signals, it will be in platform-specific code that =
goes
beyond the C standard library.</p>

<h3 id=3D"atomics">atomics</h3>

<p>I=E2=80=99ve used the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-roug=
e">_Atomic</code> qualifier <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022/05=
/14/">in examples</a> since it helps with
conciseness, but I hardly use it in practice. In part because it has the
inconvenient effect of bleeding into APIs and ABIs. As with <code class=3D"=
language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">volatile</code>, C
is using the type system to indirectly achieve a goal. Types are not
atomic, <em>loads</em> and <em>stores</em> are atomic. Predating standardiz=
ation, C
implementations have been expressing these loads and stores using
intrinsics, functions, or macros rather than through types.</p>

<p>The <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_Atomic</code> =
qualifier provides access to the most basic and most strict
atomic operations without libc. That is, it=E2=80=99s implemented purely in=
 the
compiler. However, everything outside that involves libc, and potentially
even requires linking a special atomics library.</p>

<p>Even more, one major implementation (MSVC) still doesn=E2=80=99t support=
 C11
atomics. Anywhere I care about using C atomics, I can already use the
<a href=3D"https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/_005f_005fatomic-Builtins.htm=
l">richer set of GCC built-ins</a>, which Clang also supports. If I=E2=80=
=99m
writing code intended for Windows, I=E2=80=99ll use the <a href=3D"https://=
docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/sync/interlocked-variable-access">in=
terlocked macros</a>,
which work <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022/10/05/#four-element=
s-windows">across all the compilers</a> for that platform.</p>

<h3 id=3D"stdio">stdio</h3>

<p>Standard input and output, stdio, is perhaps the primary driving factor
for my own routing around libc. Nearly every program does some kind of
input or output, but going through stdio makes things harder.</p>

<p>To read or write a file, one must first open it, e.g. <code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fopen</code>. However,
all the implementations for one platform in particular <a href=3D"https://n=
ullprogram.com/blog/2021/12/30/">does not allow
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fopen</code> to access=
 most of the file system</a>, so using libc immediately
limits the program=E2=80=99s capabilities on that platform.</p>

<p>The standard library distinguishes between =E2=80=9Ctext=E2=80=9D and =
=E2=80=9Cbinary=E2=80=9D streams. It
makes no difference on unix-like platforms, but it does on others, where
input and output are translated. Besides destroying your data, text
streams have terrible performance. Opening everything in binary mode is a
simple enough work around, but standard input, output, and error are
opened as text streams, and there is no standard function for changing
them to binary streams.</p>

<p>When using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fread</c=
ode>, some implementations use the entire buffer as a
<a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/c-runtime-library/referenc=
e/fread#remarks">temporary work space</a>, even if it returns a length less=
 than the
entire buffer. So the following won=E2=80=99t work reliably:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"n">b=
uf</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"n">N</span><span class=3D=
"p">]</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">{</span><span c=
lass=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">};</span>
<span class=3D"n">fread</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">b=
uf</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">N</span><span class=
=3D"o">-</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span =
class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">f</span><=
span class=3D"p">);</span>
<span class=3D"n">puts</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">bu=
f</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It may print junk after the expected output because <code class=3D"langu=
age-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fread</code> overwrote the
zeroes beyond it.</p>

<p>Streams are buffered, and there=E2=80=99s no reliable access to unbuffer=
ed input
and output, such as when an application is already buffering, perhaps as a
natural consequence of how it works. There=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"languag=
e-plaintext highlighter-rouge">setvbuf</code> and <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">_IONBF</code>
(=E2=80=9Cunbuffered=E2=80=9D), but in <a href=3D"https://github.com/openss=
l/openssl/issues/3281">at least one case</a> this really just means
=E2=80=9Cone byte at a time.=E2=80=9D It=E2=80=99s common for my libc-using=
 programs to end up
with double buffering since I can=E2=80=99t reliably turn off stdio bufferi=
ng.</p>

<p>Typical implementations assume streams will be used by multiple threads,
and so every access goes through a mutex. This causes terrible performance
for small reads and writes =E2=80=94 exactly the case buffering is supposed=
 to
most <em>help</em>. Not only is this unusual, such programs are probably br=
oken
anyway =E2=80=94 oblivious to the still-present race conditions =E2=80=94 a=
nd so <strong>stdio
is optimized for the unusual, broken case at the cost of the most needed
typical case</strong>.</p>

<p>There is no reliable way to interactively input and display Unicode text=
.
The C standard makes vague concessions for dealing with =E2=80=9Cwide chara=
cters=E2=80=9D
but it=E2=80=99s useless in practice. I=E2=80=99ve tried! The most common n=
eed for me is
printing a path to standard error such that it displays properly to the
user.</p>

<p>Seek offsets are limited to <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">long</code>. Some real implementations can=E2=80=99t even
open files large than 2GiB.</p>

<p>Rather than deal with all this, I add a couple of unbuffered I/O functio=
ns
to the platform layer, then put a small buffered stream implementation in
the application which flushes to the platform layer. UTF-8 for text input
and output, and if the platform layer detects it=E2=80=99s connected to a t=
erminal
or console, it does the appropriate translation. It doesn=E2=80=99t take mu=
ch to
get something more reliable than stdio. The details are <a href=3D"https://=
nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/13/">the topic for a
future article</a>, especially since you might be wondering about
formatted output.</p>

<p>As for formatted input, <a href=3D"https://sekrit.de/webdocs/c/beginners=
-guide-away-from-scanf.html">don=E2=80=99t ever bother with <code class=3D"=
language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">scanf</code></a>.</p>

<h3 id=3D"numeric-conversion">Numeric conversion</h3>

<p>Float conversion <a href=3D"https://www.exploringbinary.com/a-better-way=
-to-convert-integers-in-david-gays-strtod/">is generally a difficult proble=
m</a>, especially if
you care about <a href=3D"https://possiblywrong.wordpress.com/2015/06/21/fl=
oating-point-round-trips/">round trips</a>. It=E2=80=99s one of the better =
and most useful
parts of libc. Though even with libc it=E2=80=99s still difficult to get th=
e
simplest or shortest round-trip representation. Also, this is an area
where changing locales can be disastrous!</p>

<p>The question is then: How much does this matter in your application=E2=
=80=99s
context? There=E2=80=99s a good chance you only need to display a rounded,
low-precision representation of a float to users =E2=80=94 perhaps displayi=
ng a
player=E2=80=99s position in a debug window, etc. Or you only need to parse
medium-precision non-integral inputs following a relatively simple format.
These are not so difficult.</p>

<p>Parsing (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">atoi</code=
>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtol</code>, <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtod</code>, etc.) requi=
res null-terminated
strings, which is generally inconvenient. These integers likely came from
something <em>not</em> null-terminated like a file, and so I need to first =
append
a null terminator. I can=E2=80=99t just feed it a token from a memory-mappe=
d file.
Even when using libc, I often write my own integer parser anyway since the
libc parsers lack an appropriate interface.</p>

<p><strong>Update</strong>: NRK points out that <a href=3D"https://lists.sr=
.ht/~skeeto/public-inbox/%3C20230218081805.z57kyrbc6xzqlnx6%40gen2.localdom=
ain%3E">unsigned integer parsing treats negative
inputs as in range</a>. This is both surprising and rarely useful.
Looking more closely at the specification, I see it is also affected by
locale. Given these revelations, I would ban without exception <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">atoi</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">atol</code>, <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtoul</code>, and <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtoull</code>, and avoid <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtol</code> and <code clas=
s=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtoll</code>.</p>

<p>Formatting integers is easy. Parsing integers within in narrow range (e.=
g.
up to a million) is easy. Parsing integers to the very limits of the
numeric type <a href=3D"https://github.com/skeeto/scratch/blob/master/parse=
rs/strtonum.c">is tricky</a> because every operation must guard
against overflow regardless of signed or unsigned. Fortunately the first
two are common and the last is rarely necessary!</p>

<h3 id=3D"random-numbers">Random numbers</h3>

<p>We have <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">rand</code>=
, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">srand</code>, and <c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RAND_MAX</code>. As <a h=
ref=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2017/09/21/">a PRNG enthusiast</a>, I
could never recommend using this under any circumstances. It=E2=80=99s a PR=
NG with
mediocre output, poor performance, and global state. <code class=3D"languag=
e-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RAND_MAX</code> being
unknown ahead of time makes it even more difficult to make effective use
of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">rand</code>. You ca=
n do better on all dimensions with just <a href=3D"https://www.pcg-random.o=
rg/download.html">a few lines of
code</a>.</p>

<p>To make matters worse, typical implementations <em>expect</em> it to be =
accessed
concurrently from multiple threads, so they wrap it in a mutex. Again, it
optimizes for the unusual, broken case =E2=80=94 threads fighting each othe=
r over
non-deterministic racy results from a deterministic PRNG =E2=80=94 at the c=
ost of
the typical, sensible case. Programs relying on that mutex are already
broken.</p>

<h3 id=3D"memory-allocation">Memory allocation</h3>

<p>Includes <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">malloc</co=
de>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">calloc</code>, <c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">realloc</code>, <code cl=
ass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">free</code>, etc. Okay, but in=
 practice
used too granularly and too much such that many C programs <a href=3D"https=
://www.rfleury.com/p/untangling-lifetimes-the-arena-allocator">are tangles =
of
lifetimes</a>. Sometimes I wish there was a standard region allocator
so that independently-written libraries could speak a common, sensible,
<a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2018/06/10/">caller-controlled</a> =
allocation interface.</p>

<p>A major standardization failure here has been not moving size computatio=
ns
into the allocators themselves. <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">calloc</code> is a start: You say how big and
how many, and it works out the total allocation, checking for overflow.
There should be more of this, even if just to <a href=3D"https://www.youtub=
e.com/watch?v=3Df4ioc8-lDc0&amp;t=3D4407s">discourage individual
allocations and encourage group allocations</a>.</p>

<p>There are some edge cases around zero sizes, like <code class=3D"languag=
e-plaintext highlighter-rouge">malloc(0)</code>, and the
standard leaves the behavior <a href=3D"https://yarchive.net/comp/linux/mal=
loc_0.html">a bit too open ended</a>. However, if your
program is so poorly structured such that it may possibly pass zero to
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">malloc</code> then you=
 have bigger problems anyway.</p>

<h3 id=3D"communication-with-the-environment">Communication with the enviro=
nment</h3>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">getenv</code> is st=
raightforward, though I=E2=80=99d prefer to just access the
environment block directly, <em>a la</em> the non-standard third argument t=
o
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">main</code>.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">exit</code> is fine=
, but <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">atexit</code> is=
 jank.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">system</code> is <a=
 href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022/02/18/">essentially useless</a> =
in practice.</p>

<h3 id=3D"sorting-and-searching">Sorting and searching</h3>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">qsort</code> is <de=
l><a href=3D"https://lists.sr.ht/~skeeto/public-inbox/%3C1676216350-sup-930=
6%40thyestes.tartarus.org%3E">fine</a></del>poor because it <a href=3D"http=
s://nullprogram.com/blog/2017/01/08/">lacks a context argument</a>.
<a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2016/09/05/">Quality varies</a>. No=
t difficult to implement from scratch if
necessary. I rarely need to sort.</p>

<p>Similar story  for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
bsearch</code>. Though if I need a binary search over an
array, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">bsearch</code> =
probably isn=E2=80=99t sufficient because I usually want to find
lower and upper bounds of a range.</p>

<h3 id=3D"multi-byte-encodings-and-wide-characters">Multi-byte encodings an=
d wide characters</h3>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mblen</code>, <code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mbtowc</code>, <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mbtowc</code>, <code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">wctomb</code>, <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">mbstowcs</code>, and <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">wcstombs</code> are
connected to the locale system and don=E2=80=99t necessarily operate on any
particular encodings like UTF-8, which makes them unreliable. This is the
case for all the other wide character functionality, which is quite a few
functions. Fortunately I only ever need wide characters on one platform in
particular, not in portable code.</p>

<p>More recently are <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">m=
brtoc16</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">c16rto=
mb</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mbrtoc32</c=
ode>, and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">c32rtomb</co=
de> where
the =E2=80=9Cwide=E2=80=9D side is specified (UTF-16, UTF-32) but not the m=
ulti-byte side.
Limited support in implementations and not particularly useful.</p>

<h3 id=3D"strings">Strings</h3>

<p>Like <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ctype.h</code>=
, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">string.h</code> is a=
nother case where everything is terrible,
and some functions are virtually always misused.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memcpy</code>, <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memmove</code>, <code clas=
s=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memset</code>, and <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memcmp</code> are fine except for=
 one issue:
it is undefined behavior to pass a null pointer to these functions, <em>eve=
n
with a zero size</em>. That=E2=80=99s ridiculous. A null pointer legitimate=
ly and
usefully points to a zero-sized object. As mentioned, even <code class=3D"l=
anguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">malloc(0)</code> is
permitted to behave this way. These functions would be fine if not for
this one defect.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strcpy</code>, <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strncpy</code>, <code clas=
s=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strcat</code>, and <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strncat</code> <a href=3D"https:/=
/nullprogram.com/blog/2021/07/30/">have no legitimate
uses</a> and their use indicates confusion. As such, any code calling
them is suspect and should receive extra scrutiny. In fact, <strong>I have =
yet
to see a single correct use of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">strncpy</code> in a real program</strong>. (Usage hint:
the length argument should refer to the destination, not the source.) When
it=E2=80=99s up to me, these functions are banned without exception. This a=
pplies
equally to non-standard versions of these functions like <code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strlcpy</code>.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strlen</code> has l=
egitimate uses, but is used too often. It should only appear
at system boundaries when receiving strings of unknown size (e.g. <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">argv</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">getenv</code>), and sh=
ould never be applied to a static string. (Hint: you can
use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sizeof</code> on t=
hose.)</p>

<p>When I see <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strchr</=
code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strcmp</code> o=
r <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strncmp</code> I won=
der why you don=E2=80=99t know the
lengths of your strings. On the other hand, <code class=3D"language-plainte=
xt highlighter-rouge">strcspn</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">strpbrk</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strrchr</code>, <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strspn</code>, and <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strstr</code> do not have <code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mem</code> equivalents, tho=
ugh
the null termination requirement hurts their usefulness.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strcoll</code> and =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strxfrm</code> depend =
on locale and so are at best niche.
Otherwise unpredictable. Avoid.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memchr</code> is fi=
ne except for the aforementioned null pointer restriction,
though it comes up less often here.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strtok</code> has h=
idden global state. Besides that, how long is the returned
token? It knew the length before it returned. You mean I have to call
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strlen</code> to find =
out? Banned.</p>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strerror</code> has=
 an obvious, simple, robust solution: return a pointer to a
static string in a lookup table corresponding to the error number. No
global state, thread-safe, re-entrant, and the returned string is good
until the program exits. Some implementations do this, but unfortunately
it=E2=80=99s not true for <a href=3D"https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?qu=
ery=3Dstrerror&amp;sektion=3D0">at least one real world implementation</a>,
which instead writes to a shared, global buffer. Hopefully you were
avoiding <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">errno</code> =
anyway.</p>

<h3 id=3D"threads">Threads</h3>

<p>Introduced in C11, but never gained significant traction. Anywhere you c=
an
use C threads you can use pthreads, which are better anyway.</p>

<p>Besides, thread creation probably belongs in the platform layer anyway.<=
/p>

<h3 id=3D"time-functions">Time functions</h3>

<p>Fairly niche, and I can=E2=80=99t remember using any of these except for=
 <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">time</code>
and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">clock</code> <a hr=
ef=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2019/04/30/">for seeding</a>.</p>

<h3 id=3D"wrap-up">Wrap-up</h3>

<p>I hand-waved away a long list of vestigial wide character functions, but
the above is pretty much all there is to the C standard library. The only
things I miss when avoiding it altogether are the math functions, and
<a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/12/">occasionally <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">setjmp</code>/<code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">longjmp</code></a>. Everything else I c=
an do better
myself, with little difficulty, starting from the platform layer.</p>

<p>All of the C implementations I had in mind above are very old. They will
rarely, if ever, <em>change</em>, just accrue. There isn=E2=80=99t a lot of=
 innovation
happening in this space, which is fine since I like stable targets. If you
<em>would</em> like to see interesting innovation, check out <a href=3D"htt=
ps://justine.lol/sizetricks/">what Cosmopolitan
Libc is up to</a>. It=E2=80=99s what I imagine C could be if it continued
evolving along practical dimensions.</p>



